    <body>
      <sec id="article-180794.s1" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Hypertension remains the single most significant worldwide health problem, affecting about a third of the entire global population and accounting for the largest number of preventable deaths, disproportionately affecting low and middle-income countries.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r4">[4]</xref> Only about a fifth of affected individuals have their blood pressures well controlled.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r5">[5]</xref> At&#x000a0;least 10% of all patients with hypertension worldwide will have true drug-resistant disease, once secondary causes, medication intolerance, and treatment compliance issues have been excluded.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r6">[6]</xref> For this challenging group of patients, renal denervation therapy offers a promising, durable, complementary therapeutic option that can significantly reduce elevated systolic blood pressures in at least two-thirds of those treated.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r15">[15]</xref></p>
        <p>Innervation of the kidneys includes both afferent and efferent fibers of the renal plexus. This plexus is a combination of fibers originating from the celiac plexus, aorticorenal ganglia, the intermesenteric plexus, the lumbar, and the least splanchnic nerves.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;Nerves from these plexuses attach along the renal artery and vein to enter the kidney.&#x000a0;Afferent innervation is essential for the recognition of nociception.&#x000a0;Little evidence exists for parasympathetic fibers or activity in the kidney, but anatomical findings include cholinergic axons in the main renal arteries, segmental arteries, and the renal pelvis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r18">[18]</xref></p>
        <p>Hypertension is associated with increased renal sympathetic nerve activity, which causes the kidneys to reabsorb more sodium and water, restricts renal arterial perfusion by promoting vasoconstriction, reduces glomerular filtration rate, and raises serum renin levels, collectively increasing blood pressure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r21">[21]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r23">[23]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r24">[24]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r25">[25]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r26">[26]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r27">[27]</xref>&#x000a0;Chronic sympathetic overactivity may also result in progressive renal impairment and cardiac hypertrophy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r28">[28]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r29">[29]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r30">[30]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r31">[31]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r32">[32]</xref>&#x000a0;Renal denervation therapy offers a unique, durable, and effective treatment that may help reduce the morbidity and mortality of otherwise uncontrollable hypertension, although cardiovascular outcomes trials are lacking.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r33">[33]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r34">[34]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r35">[35]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r36">[36]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r37">[37]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-180794.s2" sec-type="Anatomy and Physiology">
        <title>Anatomy and Physiology</title>
        <p>Renal denervation therapy involves the elimination of the kidneys' efferent sympathetic innervation, primarily by destroying adventitial and adjacent periarterial adrenergic fibers that closely surround all renal arteries, branches, and accessory vessels.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r38">[38]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r39">[39]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r40">[40]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r41">[41]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r42">[42]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r43">[43]</xref>&#x000a0;The kidney is innervated by the renal plexus, a mixed neural network composed of sympathetic, visceral afferent, and parasympathetic fibers. However, parasympathetic nerves are not found in the renal parenchyma (see <bold>Image</bold>. Kidney Innervation).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r43">[43]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r44">[44]</xref>&#x000a0;Sympathetic input to the kidney is 1 of 3 fundamental mechanisms responsible for the normal regulation of blood pressure, the other 2 being the baroreceptor-mediated response to changes in blood flow and the control of sodium chloride concentration by the macula densa cells of the juxtaglomerular apparatus.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r45">[45]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r46">[46]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r47">[47]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r48">[48]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r49">[49]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The kidneys receive sympathetic innervation from multiple sources, including the paravertebral sympathetic chain, the spinal cord (T8&#x02013;L1), the celiac and aorticorenal ganglia,&#x000a0;the least and lesser splanchnic nerves, and the upper lumbar splanchnic nerves (see <bold>Image</bold>. Renal and Adrenal Nerve Pathways).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r50">[50]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r51">[51]</xref>&#x000a0;Sympathetic preganglionic fibers originate from the paravertebral sympathetic chain, connect to the kidneys through the celiac and aorticorenal ganglia, and then form a postganglionic network of efferent sympathetic nerves in and immediately adjacent to the adventitia of the accessory, segmental, and main renal arteries of the kidney (see&#x000a0;<bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Sympathetic Chain and Trunk).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r52">[52]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r53">[53]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r54">[54]</xref></p>
        <p>The sympathetic nerve fibers to the kidneys closely surround all renal arteries and are joined at multiple sites along the length of each vessel.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r38">[38]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r39">[39]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r40">[40]</xref>&#x000a0;A significant percentage of these nerves (late-arriving nerves) skip over the proximal and middle portions of the main renal arteries or bypass them entirely and join distal branches, segmental vessels, or accessory arteries instead.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r38">[38]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r39">[39]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r40">[40]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r55">[55]</xref>&#x000a0;This explains the need for multiple ablative treatments along the full length of all accessible renal arteries of adequate size (&#x0003e;3 mm diameter), including branches, accessory, and segmental vessels, to achieve relatively complete denervation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r56">[56]</xref>&#x000a0;<bold>The most critical site for renal denervation therapy is the proximal renal nerve bed, which contains the largest concentration</bold>&#x000a0;<bold>of renal sympathetic nerves.</bold><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r39">[39]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r43">[43]</xref> However, they may be located farther from the renal artery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r39">[39]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r43">[43]</xref></p>
        <p>Some sympathetic nerves may lie outside the denervation devices' treatment range, bypass the ablated arterial segments, or travel around arterial vessels too small to access for nerve ablation, which explains why even properly performed procedures may fail.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r55">[55]</xref>&#x000a0;These sympathetic nerves innervate the intrarenal vasculature and tubules, modifying renal blood flow by increasing vasoconstriction, reducing the glomerular filtration rate, enhancing sodium and water reabsorption, and promoting renin release.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r57">[57]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r58">[58]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r59">[59]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r60">[60]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r61">[61]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r62">[62]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r63">[63]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r64">[64]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Sympathetic activation induces vasoconstriction of afferent arterioles and stimulates &#x003b2;1-adrenergic receptors on juxtaglomerular cells, triggering renin release and subsequent activation of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, which promotes sodium and water reabsorption in the nephron, thereby contributing to elevations in systemic blood pressure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r57">[57]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r58">[58]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r59">[59]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r60">[60]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r61">[61]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r62">[62]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r63">[63]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r64">[64]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r65">[65]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r66">[66]</xref></p>
        <p>Renal denervation therapy, by ablating sympathetic innervation to the kidneys, eliminates or strongly reduces these hypertension-promoting effects, thereby lowering blood pressure in most patients, but not all.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r67">[67]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r68">[68]</xref>&#x000a0;Increased sympathetic activity is also linked to conditions like chronic kidney disease, obesity, and sleep apnea in patients with hypertension.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r30">[30]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r69">[69]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r70">[70]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r71">[71]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r72">[72]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r73">[73]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r74">[74]</xref>&#x000a0;A clear understanding of the anatomy and physiology of the sympathetic nerves to the kidneys allows clinicians to guide renal denervation procedural strategy, optimize patient selection, and better anticipate therapeutic outcomes (see <bold>Image.</bold>&#x000a0;Kidney Anatomy).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r43">[43]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-180794.s3" sec-type="Indications">
        <title>Indications</title>
        <p>
<bold>Patient Selection and Clinical Considerations for Renal Denervation Procedures</bold>
</p>
        <p>Drug-resistant hypertension is defined as blood pressure exceeding recommended goal levels despite treatment with maximally tolerated doses of at least&#x000a0;3 antihypertensive medications from different drug classes, including angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors or angiotensin II receptor blockers, calcium channel blockers, and a diuretic (typically a thiazide-type).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r75">[75]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r76">[76]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r77">[77]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r78">[78]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r79">[79]</xref>&#x000a0;The white coat effect, inadequate dosing, and medication nonadherence or intolerance are common causes of uncontrolled hypertension that is not truly drug-resistant.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r80">[80]</xref>&#x000a0;All&#x000a0;causes&#x000a0;of secondary hypertension, particularly hyperaldosteronism, which is common in this patient group, must also be excluded.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r80">[80]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r81">[81]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>True drug-resistant hypertension is rare, accounting for 10.3% of all patients with high blood pressure. In contrast, uncontrolled or poorly managed hypertension due to drug intolerance, poor patient compliance, irregular dosing schedules, white coat effect, and inadequate prescribed medical treatment (pseudoresistant hypertension) is quite common.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r75">[75]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r82">[82]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r83">[83]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r84">[84]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r85">[85]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r86">[86]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r87">[87]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r88">[88]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r89">[89]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r90">[90]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r91">[91]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r92">[92]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r93">[93]</xref>&#x000a0;Compliance with antihypertensive medical therapy should be verified&#x000a0;to identify patients who are noncompliant from those who are truly drug-resistant.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r83">[83]</xref> This verification is best done in a nonconfrontational manner using a combination of several direct and indirect methods, including the following:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Take time to build trust and a strong patient-physician relationship, which will allow the patient to feel more comfortable disclosing nonadherence without fear of blame.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Direct discussions with the patient, home caregivers, spouse, and family. Please include them in treatment discussions whenever possible, as they can help the patient improve compliance.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Identify the reasons for the patient's noncompliance (eg, cost, adverse effects, mental illness, dementia, forgetfulness, lack of understanding of the clinical significance of medical treatment) so that they can be addressed.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Have the patient keep a written or electronic diary to record the date and time of medication administration. However, recognize that this is easy to circumvent or alter.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Use a nonjudgmental approach, which is often preferred. An example would be "Many people have trouble taking their medications regularly. Do you find this is the case for any of your medications?"&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Use standardized, validated questionnaires, eg, the "Brief Medication Questionnaire," the "Medication Adherence Report Scale," or the "Morisky Medication Adherence Scale."&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Advise patient caregivers or family to perform pill counts, which can be helpful but may be unreliable due to "pill dumping." Most useful if performed by someone else in the home who can verify accuracy and actual compliance.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Review refill records from the pharmacy. However, this only verifies that the medications were available and cannot confirm that they were actually ingested as prescribed.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Observe for an expected clinical response or the lack thereof.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Take a blood sample to confirm therapeutic serum drug levels.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r94">[94]</xref>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r95">[95]</xref>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r96">[96]</xref>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r97">[97]</xref>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r98">[98]</xref>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r99">[99]</xref>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r100">[100]</xref>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r101">[101]</xref>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Resolving patient compliance issues&#x000a0;regarding their antihypertensive medications</bold> can be challenging and is best done in conjunction with the patient's family and caregivers.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r99">[99]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r101">[101]</xref>&#x000a0;This topic should be approached nonconfrontationally. Taking time to build trust and a strong clinician-patient relationship will allow the patient to feel more comfortable admitting noncompliance without fear of blame.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r99">[99]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r101">[101]</xref>&#x000a0;Develop targeted strategies to address individual factors that interfere with antihypertensive medication adherence.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r99">[99]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r101">[101]</xref> Tailor solutions to the patient's individual needs, lifestyle, concerns, and problems; involve the patient and his family in creating a plan to improve compliance.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r99">[99]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r100">[100]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r101">[101]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>For example, blister packaging and prefilled medication packs or pouches organize multiple medications by date, dosage, and time of administration, thereby simplifying complex medication schedules and improving&#x000a0;medication adherence.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r100">[100]</xref>&#x000a0;Combined data from results from multiple studies suggest that about 50% of the patients who appear to have drug-resistant high blood pressure in the clinic will ultimately demonstrate pseudo-resistant hypertension on further investigation, mostly due to medication noncompliance.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r83">[83]</xref>&#x000a0;Even so, they may still be selectively treated with renal denervation therapy if they are not amenable or responsive to other remedies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r102">[102]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r103">[103]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Potential candidates for renal denervation therapy</bold> for managing drug-resistant hypertension include patients in whom contributing lifestyle factors have been addressed, and all secondary causes of hypertension have been addressed, treated, or eliminated.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r67">[67]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r68">[68]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r104">[104]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r105">[105]</xref>&#x000a0;Patients who remain noncompliant with their medications and continue to have significant hypertension despite all measures to resolve their noncompliance issues are still eligible for renal denervation therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r68">[68]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r102">[102]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r106">[106]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r107">[107]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Significant lifestyle factors and secondary causes of resistant hypertension</bold> include the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Contributing lifestyle factors include high salt intake, excessive alcohol consumption, physical inactivity, pregnancy, obesity, obstructive sleep apnea, sleep deficiency, smoking, and elevated stress levels.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r108">[108]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r109">[109]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Treatable secondary causes of drug-resistant hypertension should be identified and managed before considering renal denervation therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r110">[110]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r111">[111]</xref>&#x000a0;These&#x000a0;include aortic coarctation, chronic renal insufficiency, Cushing syndrome, hyperparathyroidism, pheochromocytoma, renovascular pathology (renal artery stenosis, fibromuscular dysplasia), toxic hyperthyroidism, and especially primary hyperaldosteronism, which may be present in up to 25% of patients with resistant hypertension.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r111">[111]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r112">[112]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r113">[113]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r114">[114]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r115">[115]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r116">[116]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r117">[117]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>A clinical trial of spironolactone or a similar mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist is recommended before considering renal denervation therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r68">[68]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r104">[104]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r118">[118]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hyperaldosteronism is initially identified by a serum aldosterone level &#x0003e;10 ng/dL in the setting of a suppressed renin (&#x0003c;1) or an aldosterone/renin ratio &#x0003e;20:1.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r112">[112]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r113">[113]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hyperaldosteronism is initially identified by a serum aldosterone above the upper limit of normal in the setting of a suppressed renin (&#x0003c;1), and typically with an aldosterone/renin ratio &#x0003e;20:1.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r68">[68]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r111">[111]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r112">[112]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r113">[113]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r114">[114]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r115">[115]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r116">[116]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r117">[117]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>In addition to hypertension unresponsive to medication, the American College of Cardiology and the American Heart Association define drug-resistant hypertension as &#x02265;130/80 mm Hg. In contrast, the European Society of Hypertension and the European Society of Cardiology define it as &#x02265;140/90 mm Hg.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r119">[119]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r120">[120]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r121">[121]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Candidates for renal denervation therapy</bold>&#x000a0;would include individuals with drug-resistant hypertension (including true-drug resistance, medication intolerance, or uncorrectable patient noncompliance), a&#x000a0;high likelihood of adherence to regular follow-up visits, and at least one of the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>High cardiovascular risk</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Patient preference for a durable or permanent reduction in blood pressure without additional medications, even if it requires a procedure&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r122">[122]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r123">[123]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r124">[124]</xref>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Multiple studies indicate that approximately 30% to 40% of patients on oral antihypertensive therapy would prefer a curative procedure over long-term medication usage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r125">[125]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r126">[126]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r127">[127]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-180794.s4" sec-type="Contraindications">
        <title>Contraindications</title>
        <p>Contraindications for renal denervation therapy&#x000a0;include the presence of abnormal renal neoplasms or hormonally active adrenal tumors, which have alternate effective treatment options.&#x000a0;Patients with iliac or femoral artery stenosis that would impede safe or reliable catheter insertion are not suitable candidates unless radial artery access is available.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r128">[128]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r129">[129]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r130">[130]</xref>&#x000a0;Pregnancy, renal artery aneurysms, renal arteries measuring less than 3 mm or greater than 8 mm in diameter, and fibromuscular dysplasia of the renal artery preclude procedural intervention.</p>
        <p>Another contraindication is atherosclerotic disease involving over 50% arterial narrowing, but treatment may be possible for selected patients with 30% to 50% stenosis.&#x000a0;Functioning adrenal or renal tumors and significant renal artery stenosis, defined as a narrowing of greater than 50%, are additional contraindications.&#x000a0;Relative contraindications include a solitary kidney, severe renal failure with a glomerular filtration rate of less than 40 mL/min, or status post kidney transplant; the presence of a previously stented renal artery poses some limitations, but denervation therapy has been successfully performed proximal and distal to renal arterial stents as long as there is at least a 5 mm gap.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r131">[131]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r132">[132]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r133">[133]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r134">[134]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-180794.s5" sec-type="Equipment">
        <title>Equipment</title>
        <p>Equipment used for this procedure includes:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Anesthesia equipment and supplies</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Endovascular radiofrequency device or ultrasound denervation catheter with a variety of balloons to optimize access to all treatable renal arteries</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Quantitative angiography or intravascular ultrasound aids in correctly sizing the artery, which is important for ultrasound denervation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hemostatic valve for the guide catheter</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Intravenous renal contrast</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Local anesthetic, needles, and syringes</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Fluoroscopy equipment</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Real-time, high-definition monitors and fluoroscopy equipment (usually already present in a cath lab)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Renal denervation generator (radiofrequency or ultrasound) and control unit</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Routine angiography supplies, such as various guide catheters, clamps, and introducer sheaths for vascular access</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Sedative medications for the patient, if needed</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Selection of guide wires (including 0.035-inch and 0.014-inch) and a variety of angiographic catheters</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Sterile drapes and antiseptic skin prep</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Sterile water and normal saline</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ultrasound to help identify the femoral artery for access in patients where this is otherwise challenging</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-180794.s6" sec-type="Personnel">
        <title>Personnel</title>
        <p>Personnel who may be involved with&#x000a0;renal denervation therapy include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Interventionalist (interventional cardiologist or radiologist)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Catheter lab nurses, technicians, and support staff</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Scrub nurse</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Technician or nurse trained to operate the generator control unit for the renal denervation device</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Anesthesia personnel</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-180794.s7" sec-type="Preparation">
        <title>Preparation</title>
        <p>
<bold>Preoperative Assessment</bold>
</p>
        <p>Preoperative assessment for renal denervation therapy begins with establishing an accurate average baseline blood pressure. Patients should record home blood pressure twice daily for at least 1 week or undergo 24-hour ambulatory monitoring, with results averaged to confirm true baseline hypertensive status and exclude white-coat hypertension.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r82">[82]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r135">[135]</xref>&#x000a0;In addition, this baseline blood pressure serves as the basis for post-procedure comparisons to assess success.&#x000a0;Imaging,&#x000a0;eg, a computed tomography angiogram, is recommended to exclude structural causes of renovascular hypertension, eg, renal artery stenosis or fibromuscular dysplasia, and to delineate renal arterial anatomy, including accessory arteries that may influence procedural outcomes and candidacy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r136">[136]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r137">[137]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r138">[138]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Accessory arteries measuring 3 to 8 mm should be treated in patients undergoing renal denervation, if feasible.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r139">[139]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r140">[140]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r141">[141]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r142">[142]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r143">[143]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r144">[144]</xref>&#x000a0;The presence of multiple small accessory arteries or a short main renal artery (&#x0003c;20 mm) will increase procedural duration and complexity and increase the risk of reduced benefit due to incomplete sympathetic denervation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r145">[145]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r146">[146]</xref>&#x000a0;Contrast-induced nephropathy is a potential risk, particularly in patients with preexisting renal insufficiency, as renal denervation procedures require radioopaque dye for optimal visualization.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r147">[147]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Further, contrast and ionizing radiation exposure should be minimized, and hydration status optimized.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r148">[148]</xref>&#x000a0;Diluted contrast (60% contrast/40% saline) and preoperative hydration with parenteral normal saline are recommended to minimize nephrotoxicity, regardless of baseline kidney function.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r148">[148]</xref>&#x000a0;Alternative imaging modalities include carbon dioxide angiography for procedural guidance, duplex ultrasonography, and noncontrast-enhanced magnetic resonance angiography for preprocedural assessment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r148">[148]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r149">[149]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r150">[150]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r151">[151]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r152">[152]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r153">[153]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r154">[154]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r155">[155]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r156">[156]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r157">[157]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r158">[158]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r159">[159]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r160">[160]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r161">[161]</xref>&#x000a0;Adequate analgesia or sedation is required&#x000a0;to manage patient discomfort caused by somatic nerve stimulation during the procedure. Anticoagulation with aspirin loading and intraoperative unfractionated heparin is recommended, followed by optional low-dose aspirin for 30 days postprocedure to reduce the risk of thrombus formation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r162">[162]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r163">[163]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-180794.s8" sec-type="Technique or Treatment">
        <title>Technique or Treatment</title>
        <p>Renal denervation therapy has proven to be an extremely safe procedure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r164">[164]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r165">[165]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r166">[166]</xref>&#x000a0;A pooled analysis of recent randomized sham-controlled trials involving 293 patients demonstrated major adverse events in only 3 patients (1% of all individuals treated).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r167">[167]</xref>&#x000a0;Renal denervation is generally limited to patients with an estimated glomerular filtration rate (GFR) above 40 mL/min, reflecting the absence of safety data in patients with significant renal functional impairment rather than any increased risk of kidney failure or reduced efficacy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r121">[121]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r168">[168]</xref>&#x000a0;Those with kidney failure and a GFR less than 40 mL/min should ideally enter a clinical trial. Nevertheless, they may be considered for renal denervation therapy on an individual basis if other treatment options are ineffective or unavailable.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r70">[70]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r148">[148]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r169">[169]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r170">[170]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r171">[171]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r172">[172]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r173">[173]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r174">[174]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r175">[175]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r176">[176]</xref></p>
        <p>Patients at elevated cardiovascular risk, including those with coronary artery disease, diabetes, prior transient ischemic attack, history of strokes, or chronic renal insufficiency, may derive the greatest benefit from antihypertensive renal denervation therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r104">[104]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r105">[105]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r148">[148]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r162">[162]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r169">[169]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r177">[177]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r178">[178]</xref>&#x000a0;Such decisions are best made through shared decision-making procedures and in consultation with an interprofessional team comprising hypertension physicians, an interventional cardiologist, and, especially in chronic kidney disease, a nephrologist.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r179">[179]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r180">[180]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r181">[181]</xref>&#x000a0;<bold>A systematic review and meta-analysis identified the clinical factors that suggest a good clinical response</bold>, <bold>which include the following:</bold>&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Decreased cardiac baroreflex sensitivity</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Greater variability in blood pressure readings</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Higher average heart rate</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Higher average baseline blood pressure</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Lower pulse wave velocity</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Orthostatic hypertension&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r141">[141]</xref>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r167">[167]</xref>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r170">[170]</xref>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r182">[182]</xref>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r183">[183]</xref>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r170">[170]</xref>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r184">[184]</xref>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r185">[185]</xref>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r186">[186]</xref>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r187">[187]</xref>
<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r188">[188]</xref>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The only consistent predictor of a favorable clinical response to renal denervation therapy across numerous studies is a higher average baseline blood pressure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r167">[167]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r182">[182]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r183">[183]</xref>&#x000a0;In fact, patients with higher baseline hypertension tend to demonstrate the greatest absolute reduction in their blood pressures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r189">[189]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r190">[190]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Common Factors in Performing Renal Denervation Therapy Procedures</bold>
</p>
        <p>Common factors that guide renal denervation procedures require careful preparation, structured vascular access, and precise intravascular technique. Patients fast before the procedure, and clinicians follow routine preoperative precautions in accordance with standard surgical protocols, including withholding specific anticoagulants and glucagon-like peptide 1 receptor agonists. Patients continue their usual antihypertensive therapies.</p>
        <p>Procedural discomfort often stems from somatic nerve stimulation, which is typically severe but short-lived; therefore, clinicians should administer appropriate analgesia, conscious sedation, or anesthesia, with conscious sedation meeting the needs of most patients. An analgesic bolus is administered 3 minutes before the first ablative treatment. Supplemental hydration with normal saline benefits all patients, regardless of renal function, to reduce the risk of contrast-induced nephropathy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r148">[148]</xref> Infusion rates are determined by left ventricular ejection fraction: 0.5 mL/kg/hour for patients with a left ventricular ejection fraction less than 40% and 1 mL/kg/hour otherwise.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r148">[148]</xref></p>
        <p>Patients with extremely high blood pressures may require continuous intravenous sodium nitroprusside or nitrate infusions to maintain hemodynamic stability during the procedure. Unfractionated heparin provides anticoagulation, targeting an activated clotting time greater than 250 seconds. Clinicians generally employ a standard femoral access approach, advance a 0.035-inch guidewire, and position a 6 French pigtail catheter. The catheter tip is placed over the interspace between the first and second lumbar vertebrae for an aortogram to identify accessory renal vessels arising from the aorta. Significant renal failure may prompt omitting the aortogram, though doing so may leave accessory vessels unrecognized. An internal mammary or renal double-curve catheter typically supports renal arterial access and engagement of the main renal arteries and clinically relevant accessory branches.</p>
        <p>Selective arteriography is then performed, using 2 to 3 mL of diluted contrast (60% contrast/40% normal saline) for most renal arteries. Selective, limited-use fluoroscopy can lower radiation exposure for patients and staff without diminishing visualization. Carbon dioxide angiography may be used in cases of severe contrast allergy or significant renal failure, though image quality remains inferior. Clinicians carefully evaluate the diameter, origin, course, tortuosity, and branching patterns of the renal arteries, with particular attention to accessory vessels and abnormalities. The access catheter tip is advanced 3 to 4 mm into the renal artery to provide stability and permit introduction of the denervation device.</p>
        <p>Renal denervation therapy involves bilateral ablations in all main and accessory renal arteries measuring 3 mm or greater.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r139">[139]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r140">[140]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r141">[141]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r142">[142]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r143">[143]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r144">[144]</xref> Multiple ablations proceed from distal to proximal segments, generally avoiding treatment within 5 mm of the arterial ostium.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r56">[56]</xref> Intra-arterial vasodilator use before ablation remains optional, as current evidence does not confirm a clinical benefit.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r191">[191]</xref> Nicardipine (200 &#x000b5;g), nitroglycerin (200&#x02013;400 &#x000b5;g), nitroprusside (100 &#x000b5;g), and verapamil (100 &#x000b5;g) can theoretically support selective angiography and reduce vasospasm, although their efficacy remains uncertain.</p>
        <p>Additional intra-arterial vasodilators may be administered at the end of the procedure to facilitate identification of vascular trauma or leakage on a final selective arteriogram. Immediate postprocedural renal arterial vasospasm commonly appears after denervation therapy but resolves without intervention, and current evidence does not clarify whether vasodilators meaningfully reduce this response.</p>
        <p>Clinicians perform a post-procedure angiogram and keep a guidewire in place until the angiogram is thoroughly reviewed and approved. Bilateral therapy maximizes effectiveness in patients with&#x000a0;2 functioning kidneys.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r165">[165]</xref> The procedure typically lasts 45 to 60 minutes, although multiple accessory vessels may extend the total time. Most patients undergo renal denervation on an outpatient basis and return home on the same day.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Radiofrequency Renal Denervation Therapy&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>Radiofrequency renal denervation therapy targets all renal arterial vessels with diameters 3 mm or greater located outside the renal parenchyma, with some recommendations supporting treatment of segmental branches (see&#x000a0;<bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Radiofrequency Renal Denervation). The device measures 4 French and advances over a 0.014-inch guide wire, introduced through a 6 French sheath. Once positioned, the guide wire is retracted from the catheter tip, allowing the radiofrequency catheter to assume its operating helical configuration.</p>
        <p>No intraparenchymal treatments should be performed. Each catheter functions as a spiral electromagnetic emitter equipped with four sequentially activated electrodes that generate a circular ablative pattern along a spiral trajectory.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r192">[192]</xref> In narrow or bifurcating regions, operators may deactivate one or more electrodes when contact problems or overheating arise.</p>
        <p>Additional guide wires may help straighten tortuous arteries to assist device insertion, but all auxiliary wires must be removed before ablative therapy. Retracting the guide wire allows the spiral tip to assume its functional helical shape. Reinserting the wire straightens the device for repositioning, and retracting it permits the helix to reform. Early renal arterial bifurcations warrant treatment of all branches. When adequate contact proves difficult, rotating the device 90 degrees clockwise or counterclockwise often resolves the issue.&#x000a0;A single radiofrequency catheter is typically used to treat renal arterial vessels measuring 3 to 8 mm in diameter.</p>
        <p>Starting impedance generally ranges from 220 &#x003a9; to 250 &#x003a9;, with higher values suggesting improved contact between the helical catheter tip and the arterial wall or positioning within a smaller vessel. Impedance variations of 10 to 20 &#x003a9; or more signal device instability and require repositioning, while an impedance drop greater than 10% is expected during ablation. High-impedance readings reflect good wall contact but may also indicate placement in a smaller vessel or a side branch. Smaller arterial branches carry a higher risk of electrode overheating, which triggers automatic device shutdown. Cooling improves when 2 or even 3 electrodes are deactivated. After initial treatment, previously switched-off electrodes can be activated to complete therapy.</p>
        <p>Complete ablative denervation requires each radiofrequency application to run for at least 45 seconds, a duration automatically controlled by the system. Treatments shorter than 45 seconds should be repeated. Overheating may interrupt therapy; activating fewer electrodes or using only one electrode, then sequentially rotating through the others, often resolves this issue. Loss of electrode contact is a frequent cause of treatment failure, particularly when an electrode lies near a branching ostium.</p>
        <p>Rotating the device 90 degrees often restores contact. When these strategies fail, a quick selective angiogram may clarify the underlying problem (see <bold>Image</bold>. Concurrent Angiogram). A total of 4 to 12 ablative treatments is recommended for each treatable artery, with greater numbers associated with improved efficacy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r193">[193]</xref> Periodic flushing of the guide catheter with heparinized saline helps prevent thrombus formation throughout the procedure.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Ultrasonic Renal Denervation Therapy&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>Ultrasonic renal denervation therapy uses an inflatable balloon catheter positioned within the renal artery, with the balloon expanded to maintain full contact with the arterial wall. The device measures 6 French and advances over a 0.014-inch guide wire, which is introduced through a 7- or 8 French femoral sheath. A standard hemostatic valve attaches to the guide catheter to permit advancement of the ultrasonic denervation device. Only the main renal artery and any significant accessory vessels are treated. Ablation begins distally, just proximal to the first branching or bifurcation of the renal artery.</p>
        <p>The ultrasonic transducer does not directly contact the arterial wall. Balloon sizes accommodate arteries measuring 3.5, 4.2, 5, 6, 7, and 8 mm in diameter, and a 5 mm radiopaque marker centered on the balloon shaft assists with precise positioning. Balloon pressure remains minimal and is automatically controlled. Treatment settings are automatically adjusted based on balloon size to optimize sympathetic nerve disruption. Sterile water, continuously cooled and circulated through the balloon, preserves arterial wall cooling before, during, and immediately after therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r194">[194]</xref>&#x000a0;Each treatable artery typically undergoes&#x000a0;2 or, optimally,&#x000a0;3 separate 7-second ablative sonications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r195">[195]</xref> After a short cooling interval, the balloon automatically deflates, enabling repositioning for additional treatments. Multiple balloon sizes may be required for arteries of varying diameters.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Alcohol-Mediated Denervation</bold>
</p>
        <p>Alcohol-mediated renal denervation uses chemical neurolysis to ablate sympathetic renal nerves supplying the major renal arteries and clinically&#x000a0;significant accessory vessels.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r196">[196]</xref> This&#x000a0;non&#x02013;Food and Drug Administration&#x02013;approved approach has limited clinical data; however, several studies report substantial reductions in blood pressure, whereas others do not.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r196">[196]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r197">[197]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r198">[198]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r199">[199]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r200">[200]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r201">[201]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r202">[202]</xref> Because only a single midarterial treatment is delivered to each treatable artery, this method may benefit patients with unusually short renal arteries or accessory vessels that cannot accommodate radiofrequency or ultrasonic systems.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r196">[196]</xref></p>
        <p>Dehydrated alcohol injected into the perivascular space produces chemical neurolysis of the renal sympathetic nerves. A 7 French guiding catheter supports device placement. Treatable vessels measure 3 to 7 mm in diameter. The treatment catheter positions the target treatment zone at the arterial midpoint and at least 1 cm from the renal parenchyma. The device deploys 3 microneedles (0.008 inch), spaced 120 degrees apart, which penetrate approximately 3.2 mm into the arterial adventitia and perivascular space. Renal denervation involves injecting 0.6 mL of dehydrated alcohol into the adventitia and perivascular tissues, with a maximum total dose of 2.4 mL per patient. Accessory vessels meeting the 3 mm or greater diameter requirement may be treated using the same technique.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Radial Artery Approaches For Denervation Therapy</bold>
</p>
        <p>Radial artery approaches for renal denervation therapy are&#x000a0;in development. This strategy offers several advantages, including reduced access-site complications, lower bleeding risk, improved patient comfort, earlier postprocedural mobility, no additional capital investment, and easier anatomical access. The technique also provides a viable alternative for patients with peripheral artery disease, limited femoral artery access, or elevated bleeding risk.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r128">[128]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r203">[203]</xref>&#x000a0;Catheters designed for radial access must be longer (156 cm compared to the standard femoral length of 117 cm) and narrower than those used for femoral access. They may require additional operator training and expertise. This approach is most suitable for smaller, shorter patients.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Procedure Summary and Key Considerations&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>The following key factors should be kept in mind when performing&#x000a0;renal denervation therapy procedures:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Patients should fast before renal denervation therapy, as sedation and analgesia are used to minimize pain associated with denervation therapy.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Denervation therapy should not be done in vessels within the renal parenchyma.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Femoral arterial access is typically obtained, followed by aortography and selective renal angiography to confirm the number and precise anatomy of all significant arterial vessels supplying the kidneys.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Radial artery approaches are under development for both radiofrequency and ultrasonic renal denervation devices, with the potential to improve procedural efficiency, patient comfort, and reduce complications.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Potential candidates must be intolerant of medical treatment or fail at least 3 or more drug therapies (including at least&#x000a0;one diuretic) for their hypertension, and all secondary causes, such as primary hyperaldosteronism, should be ruled out.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Optimal individuals will have renal arteries at least 20 mm in length and 3 mm to 8 mm in diameter, treatable accessory arteries, a GFR of 40 to 45 mL/min, and confirmed ambulatory (out-of-office, home)&#x000a0;hypertension.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Results from radiofrequency and ultrasound-based renal denervation are comparable. Selection depends on the interventional clinician's preference, training, and equipment availability.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Shared decision-making is essential, as up to one-third of patients may not enjoy a beneficial response, and many other patients treated successfully will still need to take antihypertensive medications.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Device-specific approaches</bold>
<bold>vary</bold>, as follows:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>The radiofrequency device is designed to deliver multiple bilateral ablations (4 to 12) to all significant accessible renal arterial branches outside the renal parenchyma, as well as to the main and any accessory renal arteries measuring greater than 3 mm in diameter.
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Each ablative treatment typically lasts 1 minute but may be extended to 2 minutes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r204">[204]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r205">[205]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r206">[206]</xref></p></list-item><list-item><p>This can be achieved with a single radiofrequency device.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The ultrasonic device&#x000a0;should deliver at least 2, and optimally 3, separate sonications to each main renal artery and to any accessory or segmental arteries greater than 3 mm in diameter, without targeting arterial branches.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Each ablative treatment takes only 7 seconds.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r195">[195]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>This may require several devices, as balloon sizes vary with the diameter of the arterial vessels being treated.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The overall procedure duration for both devices is typically 45 to 60 minutes, depending on anatomical complexity. Discomfort during ablative energy delivery comes from somatic nerve stimulation and is often severe but generally transient. Most procedures can be performed on an outpatient basis with conscious sedation.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-180794.s9" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>The most common complication was treatment failure (no significant reduction in blood pressure), followed by renal arterial perforation or dissection, bradycardia, post-procedural bleeding from the groin access site, and device entrapment in a small number of patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r207">[207]</xref>&#x000a0;Complications of renal denervation therapy are rare, but include the following:&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r207">[207]</xref></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Allergic reaction to intravenous contrast</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Aortic dissection</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Bleeding from the groin access site</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Bradycardia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Cardiac arrhythmias</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Contrast-related acute kidney injury and nephropathy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Device entrapment</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Disorders associated with femoral artery vascular access (arteriovenous fistulas, bruising, hematomas, pseudoaneurysms, etc.)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hematuria</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Inadequate renal sympathetic nerve ablation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Nausea or vomiting</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pain from the femoral access site and/or the kidneys</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Regrowth of functional renal sympathetic nerves over time</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Renal artery dissection, perforation, or stenosis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Retroperitoneal bleeding</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Subcapsular renal hematoma</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Treatment failure</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-180794.s10" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>
<bold>Hypertension is a Major Worldwide Health Problem</bold>
</p>
        <p>Hypertension represents a major global health concern, affecting one-third of the entire adult population or an estimated 1.56 billion individuals worldwide, with about two-thirds in low and middle-income countries, and its prevalence continues to rise.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;The World Health Organization (WHO) estimates that 46% of those affected are unaware they even have the disorder, and less than half (42%) receive treatment. In contrast, only a minority of affected patients have controlled hypertension. At least 10% of all known hypertensives will have true drug-resistant high blood pressure, defined as elevated blood pressure despite at least&#x000a0;three separate antihypertensives at maximum tolerable doses after secondary causes have been excluded, or patient noncompliance with treatment that is not correctable.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;These are the patients who could primarily benefit from denervation therapy.</p>
        <p>High systolic blood pressure is the leading cause of preventable worldwide mortality, as only 21% of all adults with hypertension have their disease well-controlled (in some countries, &#x0003c;10%).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r208">[208]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r209">[209]</xref>&#x000a0;In 2019, high systolic blood pressure accounted for 19.2% (10.8 million) of all deaths worldwide.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r208">[208]</xref>&#x000a0;In China, for example, about 25% of the adult population is hypertensive, with only 16.8% being controlled.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r210">[210]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r211">[211]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r212">[212]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r213">[213]</xref>&#x000a0;Conversely, in Africa,&#x000a0;54% of all adult Africans are hypertensive, with only 7% being controlled, and the incidence is growing in both.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r214">[214]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r215">[215]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r216">[216]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r217">[217]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>In the United States, only about 23% of the nearly 120 million hypertensive adults achieve optimal blood pressure control (&#x0003c;130/80 mm Hg), whereas nearly 45% exhibit poorly controlled blood pressure despite treatment (&#x02265;140/90 mm Hg).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r67">[67]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r218">[218]</xref>&#x000a0;Noncompliance (partial or complete) or intolerance of antihypertensive medications is present in up to 40% of patients, often due to factors including complex polypharmacy regimens, adverse effects, inadequate clinical monitoring, psychiatric conditions, limited social support, inadequate prescribed therapy (ie, clinical inertia), or coexisting medical disorders.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r81">[81]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r219">[219]</xref>&#x000a0;These factors highlight a substantial unmet need for effective strategies to control blood pressure, with many patients potentially eligible for renal denervation therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r33">[33]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Renal Denervation for Drug-Resistant Hypertension</bold>
</p>
        <p>Renal sympathetic fibers are primarily located in the adventitia of the renal arteries, including accessory vessels.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r38">[38]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r192">[192]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r220">[220]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r221">[221]</xref>&#x000a0;Nerve fibers are concentrated on the superior (&#x0003e;95%) and inferior (&#x0003e;90%) aspects of the renal arteries, with occasional diagonal interconnections.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r222">[222]</xref>&#x000a0;Effective renal denervation procedures should therefore target these regions and all renal arteries that are not embedded within the renal parenchyma and measure at least 3 mm in diameter.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r139">[139]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r140">[140]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r141">[141]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>When the main renal artery bifurcates, 94% of the renal sympathetic perivascular nerves are within 3 mm of the lumens of the distal arterial branches. Still, only 46% are this close in the proximal arterial segment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r223">[223]</xref>&#x000a0;Main renal arteries with shorter proximal lengths before the first bifurcation tend to have a higher incidence of late-arriving sympathetic nerves.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r224">[224]</xref>&#x000a0;The majority (63%)&#x000a0;of the kidney's sympathetic nerves join the renal artery distal to the first arterial bifurcation, but some "late-arriving" sympathetic nerves do not merge with the kidneys' vasculature until very distal arterial segments and branches.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r38">[38]</xref></p>
        <p>Histological studies have shown that more than 90% of sympathetic nerves to the kidney lie within 7.1 mm of the proximal renal artery lumen, whereas this decreases to 2.4 mm for distal arterial segments.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r39">[39]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r223">[223]</xref>&#x000a0;The overall average distance of renal sympathetic nerves from the center of the main renal artery lumen, expressed as percentiles, is as follows:&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r39">[39]</xref></p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>50th percentile: 2.44 mm</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>75th percentile: 4.28 mm</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>90th percentile: 6.39 mm&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Taken together, these facts explain why multiple treatments along the renal arteries are recommended for effective renal denervation procedures. Available devices for renal denervation are typically effective within 6 to 6.5 mm of the renal artery lumen, with ultrasound having slightly greater penetration depth.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r223">[223]</xref>&#x000a0;More reliability, greater efficacy, and less outcome variability in radiofrequency renal denervation therapy are achieved by treating the main renal arteries, their primary bifurcation branches, significant distal branches, and accessory vessels with diameters of at least 3 mm.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r56">[56]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Results from studies of renal denervation therapy indicate that most patients with mild-to-moderate hypertension achieve a mean reduction in systolic blood pressure of approximately 10 mm Hg, with larger decreases observed in patients with higher baseline systolic blood pressure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r225">[225]</xref>&#x000a0;These reductions have been associated with significant decreases in cardiovascular risk.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r226">[226]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r227">[227]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r228">[228]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r229">[229]</xref>&#x000a0;In a large-scale analysis of randomized clinical trials, a 5 mm Hg reduction in mean systolic blood pressure was associated with approximately a 10% reduction in the risk of major cardiovascular events.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r230">[230]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Given the lack of cardiovascular outcomes data, one retrospective analysis of patients who underwent radiofrequency renal denervation compared cardiovascular outcomes, including the mortality of these patients, with expected outcomes in such patients. A lower rate of cardiovascular complications, predominantly a reduction in stroke, was seen in patients who underwent renal denervation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r231">[231]</xref>&#x000a0;Long-term efficacy has been demonstrated in hundreds of patients for up to 10 years, and observational data suggest that blood pressure will continue to decline over time.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r232">[232]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r233">[233]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r234">[234]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r235">[235]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r236">[236]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r237">[237]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r238">[238]</xref>&#x000a0;The data also indicate that clinically significant functional&#x000a0;regeneration of the sympathetic renal nerve<italic toggle="yes">&#x000a0;</italic>is unlikely.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r238">[238]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>In addition to improved blood pressure control and cardiovascular risk reduction, renal denervation therapy may confer benefits in metabolic health, insulin sensitivity, diastolic dysfunction, conditions associated with sympathetic overactivity, left ventricular hypertrophy, atrial fibrillation, cardiac arrhythmias, renal function, and overall quality of life by reducing the need for medications and lifestyle modifications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r239">[239]</xref>&#x000a0;Results<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>from some early studies on renal denervation therapy, eg, the 2014 randomized, sham-controlled Symplicity HTN-3 trial, failed to demonstrate any significant benefit of the procedure, in part&#x000a0;because of a large placebo effect in the sham-control group and a premature assessment of efficacy at only 6 months following randomization.<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/Keywords/UserViewPopup/182029#link_30087099">[6]</ext-link><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r240">[240]</xref>&#x000a0;However, a longer-term (3-year) follow-up of the same patients from the HTN-3 trial demonstrated a greater reduction in abnormal blood pressure and superior hypertensive control than in those who received only sham treatment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r241">[241]</xref>&#x000a0;Explanations for this failure, when so many other sham-controlled randomized studies clearly showed a significant benefit, include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Incomplete denervation due to inadequate procedural technique, failure to treat significant accessory vessels and distal branches, and inexperienced interventionalists&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r182">[182]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r242">[242]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r243">[243]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Postprocedure modifications in antihypertensive medications involved at least 40% of participants in each trial arm in some studies&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r182">[182]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r244">[244]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Failure to establish a proper baseline hypertension level, which did not include average ambulatory blood pressures&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r182">[182]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r244">[244]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Statistical issues such as regression to the mean, in which extreme measurements tend to approach the average over time&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r9">[9]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Inadequate length of follow-up at 6 months (the treated group showed improved blood pressure control when followed out to 36 months)&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r241">[241]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Since then, sympathetic renal nerve ablation using ultrasound, radiofrequency, or chemical agents has demonstrated efficacy and durability in numerous&#x000a0;randomized clinical trials.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r245">[245]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r246">[246]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r247">[247]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r248">[248]</xref>&#x000a0;Results from multiple sham-controlled randomized studies have shown a significant, durable positive response to renal denervation therapy, defined as at least a 5 mm Hg drop in mean systolic blood pressure, in 66% to 75% of patients, with over 50% of treated subjects able to achieve a decrease of 10 mm Hg or more, and up to one-third not responding.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r33">[33]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r102">[102]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r167">[167]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r195">[195]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r241">[241]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r245">[245]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r246">[246]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r247">[247]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r249">[249]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r250">[250]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r251">[251]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r252">[252]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r253">[253]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r254">[254]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r255">[255]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r256">[256]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r257">[257]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r258">[258]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r259">[259]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r260">[260]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r261">[261]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r262">[262]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r263">[263]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r264">[264]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r265">[265]</xref></p>
        <p>Overall, in results from sham-controlled trials, most appropriately selected patients treated with renal denervation therapy showed an average reduction of approximately 8 mm Hg in systolic blood pressure, which continued to improve over at least 72 months, with some observational studies showing an extended benefit out to 10 years.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r167">[167]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r170">[170]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r189">[189]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r195">[195]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r232">[232]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r233">[233]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r234">[234]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r237">[237]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r246">[246]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r249">[249]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r255">[255]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r257">[257]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r264">[264]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r266">[266]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r267">[267]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r268">[268]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r269">[269]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r270">[270]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r271">[271]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r272">[272]</xref>&#x000a0;A treatment failure rate of up to one-third is reported. The reason for this may involve anatomical issues (small accessory arteries or branches less than 3 mm in diameter, short main renal arteries), failure to treat the entire length of accessible renal arteries, an inadequate number of ablative treatment points (especially distal), and anatomical variations where the efferent sympathetic renal nerves are located outside the effective range of endovascular denervation devices.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r39">[39]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r273">[273]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r274">[274]</xref>&#x000a0;Renal denervation procedures employing sympathomodulatory ablative techniques are emerging as a therapeutic option for pharmacologically resistant hypertension, particularly following the Food and Drug Administration's (FDA) approval of radiofrequency- and ultrasound-based denervation devices in November 2023.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r249">[249]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r275">[275]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r276">[276]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r277">[277]</xref>&#x000a0;Unlike historical surgical sympathectomy approaches, these methods are designed to reduce blood pressure without inducing postural hypotension.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Renal denervation therapy for drug-resistant hypertension remains the only permanent means of blood pressure reduction with no adverse&#x000a0;effects. However, concomitant drug therapy is usually needed to achieve optimal hypertension control.&#x000a0;</bold>Renal denervation therapy is currently implemented in at least 60 countries, suggesting the potential for widespread adoption in the coming years.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;Contemporary renal denervation therapy appears relatively safe, with no significant&#x000a0;adverse effects, and does not appear to increase the long-term incidence of renal artery stenosis or impair renal function.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r164">[164]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Renal denervation therapy is currently recommended per guidelines</bold> (from&#x000a0;the American Heart Association, the Canadian Hypertension Specialists Society, the National Kidney Foundation, the FDA, the European Society for Hypertension, the European Cardiology Society, the Society for Cardiovascular Angiography and Interventions,&#x000a0;and many other national professional medical organizations around the world)&#x000a0;as an effeective adjunctive treatment for individuals with hypertension who are intolerant of or unresponsive to lifestyle modifications and multiple (at least 3) antihypertensive medications, particularly in the presence of elevated cardiovascular risk.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r67">[67]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r148">[148]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r162">[162]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r177">[177]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r278">[278]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r279">[279]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r280">[280]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r281">[281]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r282">[282]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r283">[283]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r284">[284]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r285">[285]</xref>&#x000a0;Up to about a third of all adults with hypertension in the United States, totaling over 35 million individuals, may qualify as potential candidates for renal denervation therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>Shared decision-making protocols should be employed before recommending renal denervation procedures. Patients should understand that approximately two-thirds to three-fourths of treated individuals achieve a durable reduction in mean systolic blood pressure of roughly 10 mm Hg (8&#x02013;15 mm Hg below baseline as measured in clinic).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r250">[250]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r286">[286]</xref>&#x000a0;Patients should maintain realistic expectations regarding outcomes, which cannot be guaranteed, as up to a third of treated individuals may not respond, and even successfully treated patients may need to continue taking antihypertensive medications for optimal results. Although complications are rare, patients should be informed of their potential.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r265">[265]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Renal Denervation Therapy in Patients with Chronic Renal Failure or on Dialysis</bold>
</p>
        <p>Renal denervation therapy is not currently recommended in international guidelines for use in patients with chronic renal failure&#x000a0;(GFR&#x0003c;40 mL/min) or on dialysis, primarily due to the exclusion of such patients from the randomized, sham-controlled trials.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r148">[148]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r162">[162]</xref>&#x000a0;High-quality, large randomized controlled trials of renal denervation therapy in these patients are minimal; however, available clinical studies suggest potential benefits in select groups.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r70">[70]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r148">[148]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r169">[169]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r170">[170]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r171">[171]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Patients with significant chronic kidney disease demonstrate increased sympathetic nervous system activity, higher renin levels, less nitric oxide (a blood pressure-lowering agent), and double the normal serum catecholamine titers compared to healthy individuals, which exacerbates their hypertension and worsens their overall cardiovascular risk.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r148">[148]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r287">[287]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r288">[288]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r289">[289]</xref>&#x000a0;Increased sympathetic nerve activity promotes inflammatory processes that drive the progression of chronic kidney disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r148">[148]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r290">[290]</xref>&#x000a0;Sympathetic nerve activity can be reduced by several antihypertensive medications, eg, angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, angiotensin II receptor blockers, and beta-blockers, but not by calcium channel blockers.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r148">[148]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r291">[291]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r292">[292]</xref></p>
        <p>Renal denervation has been shown to decrease serum renin and aldosterone levels, with a more pronounced blood pressure-lowering effect in patients with higher baseline plasma renin (&#x0003e;0.65&#x000a0;ng/mL/h).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r69">[69]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r148">[148]</xref>&#x000a0;Renal denervation also increases renalase levels, which are reduced in patients with significant renal failure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r293">[293]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r294">[294]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r295">[295]</xref>&#x000a0;Renalase is a unique monoamine oxidase flavoprotein produced by the kidneys, discovered in 2005, that helps lower blood pressure and modulates cardiac function by metabolizing catecholamines.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r294">[294]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r295">[295]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r296">[296]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r297">[297]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r298">[298]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r299">[299]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r300">[300]</xref></p>
        <p>A systematic review and meta-analysis indicated that patients with significant chronic kidney disease may actually be optimal candidates for renal denervation therapy, as it not only reduces their blood pressures but also appears to stabilize their renal function, GFR, and serum creatinine levels in patients with otherwise unresponsive drug-resistant hypertension, with the benefit lasting at least&#x000a0;3 years.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r70">[70]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r169">[169]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r170">[170]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r172">[172]</xref>&#x000a0;Another meta-analysis showed that after renal denervation therapy, patients with significant chronic kidney disease demonstrated significant (25%) reductions in albuminuria as well as preservation of their renal function over a follow-up period of&#x000a0;1 to&#x000a0;3 years.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r148">[148]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r301">[301]</xref>&#x000a0;Even patients on dialysis can benefit from renal denervation therapy to help control their drug-resistant hypertension.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r70">[70]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r148">[148]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r165">[165]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r171">[171]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r302">[302]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r303">[303]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r304">[304]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r305">[305]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r306">[306]</xref></p>
        <p>No significant decrease in GFR or kidney function has been observed in patients with chronic kidney disease and a GFR&#x000a0; of less than 40 mL/min who have undergone renal denervation therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r70">[70]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r148">[148]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r169">[169]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r173">[173]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r174">[174]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r175">[175]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r176">[176]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r303">[303]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r307">[307]</xref>&#x000a0;Available data demonstrate strong safety and efficacy in patients with a GFR less than 40 mL/min, including those receiving dialysis, suggesting that future practice will no longer regard kidney failure or insufficiency as a contraindication to this procedure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r148">[148]</xref> For now, renal denervation therapy should ideally be offered to this population within a clinical trial setting or through shared decision-making with the patient, conducted in collaboration with an interprofessional hypertension management team.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r148">[148]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Treatment recommendations for&#x000a0;patients with chronic renal failure</bold>
</p>
        <p>The following special precautions and techniques are recommended for treating patients with chronic renal failure (GFR &#x0003c;40 mL/min):</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Confirm clinic and ambulatory blood pressures&#x000a0;1 week before denervation therapy.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Verify patient compliance with antihypertensive medications.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Use shared decision-making strategies with the patient and family, given the additional risks (including contrast-induced nephropathy) and the absence of guideline recommendations.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hospitalize the patient after treatment to assess laboratory values and clinical response.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Perform a CT angiogram well before the denervation procedure to help with planning.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Use the minimum volume of diluted contrast required to perform the therapy safely.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Take every precaution to minimize the risk of contrast nephropathy.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Advise the patient to continue their regular antihypertensive medications before the denervation therapy.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Use antiplatelet therapy for&#x000a0;30 days after the procedure.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Advise patients to take their antihypertensive medications as usual before the procedure and to receive timely, effective antiplatelet therapy.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Closely monitor vital signs, including blood pressure and heart rate, immediately after denervation.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Measure serum creatinine at 24 and 72 hours after denervation.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Perform laboratory testing of blood count and renal function at 1 and 2 months after treatment.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Check office blood pressure, complete blood count, and serum creatinine at 3, 6, and 12 months after denervation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r148">[148]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r173">[173]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Renal transplantation completely disrupts the fibers of the renal plexus, abolishing sympathetic input to the kidney. The transplanted kidney retains the capacity to regulate systemic blood pressure through intrinsic autoregulatory mechanisms, including the myogenic response and macula densa-mediated sodium concentration feedback.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r308">[308]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r309">[309]</xref>&#x000a0;In the absence of sympathetic stimulation, &#x003b2;1-adrenergic receptors on juxtaglomerular cells are unable to activate the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r310">[310]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The majority of individuals with a kidney transplant, an estimated 70% to 95%, have hypertension.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r311">[311]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r312">[312]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r313">[313]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r314">[314]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r315">[315]</xref>&#x000a0;Renal denervation therapy of their native kidneys may help manage their otherwise intractable hypertension.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r171">[171]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r311">[311]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r316">[316]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r317">[317]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r318">[318]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r319">[319]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r320">[320]</xref>&#x000a0;Further research is necessary to confirm the long-term safety, efficacy, and duration of therapeutic benefit from renal denervation therapy in these challenging hypertensive patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r70">[70]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r148">[148]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r303">[303]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r307">[307]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Investigational Renal Neuromodulation Therapies for Drug-Resistant Hypertension</bold>
</p>
        <p>A laparoscopic extravascular radiofrequency device, wrapped circumferentially around the renal artery and applied under CT guidance, is under development as an alternative to renal denervation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r321">[321]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r322">[322]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r323">[323]</xref> This may be a reasonable alternative for patients who are not candidates for intravascular renal denervation due to difficulties with percutaneous femoral access or renal artery issues.&#x000a0;A combined approach for sympathetic denervation of the hepatic and renal arteries during the same procedure to better control drug-resistant hypertension is currently undergoing multinational clinical trials.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r324">[324]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r325">[325]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r326">[326]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r327">[327]</xref>&#x000a0;The rationale is that the common hepatic artery is rich in large sympathetic perivascular nerves, which current endovascular denervation devices can easily ablate.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r324">[324]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r325">[325]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r326">[326]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r327">[327]</xref></p>
        <p>Baroreceptor activation therapy uses an implanted device to stimulate the carotid and aortic arch baroreceptors.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r328">[328]</xref> This increases the baroreflex response, thereby decreasing sympathetic tone and lowering blood pressure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r329">[329]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r330">[330]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r331">[331]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r332">[332]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r333">[333]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r334">[334]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r335">[335]</xref>&#x000a0;This therapy may also be effective in patients who have failed prior renal denervation therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r336">[336]</xref>&#x000a0;Some baroreceptor devices are already FDA-approved for selected patients as a treatment for heart failure, but not specifically for blood pressure reduction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r335">[335]</xref></p>
        <p>Selective stimulation of the brainstem (nucleus tractus solitarius), the spinal cord, and the vagal nerves can affect the autonomic nervous system, reducing blood pressure in drug-resistant hypertensive individuals.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r328">[328]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r337">[337]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r338">[338]</xref>&#x000a0;Transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation can reduce sympathetic nerve activity, but its efficacy in lowering blood pressure remains unknown.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r328">[328]</xref>&#x000a0;Early studies have suggested that animals undergoing ultrasonic renal artery denervation therapy who subsequently experienced myocardial infarction showed improved survival, lower renal norepinephrine levels, better preservation of cardiac function, and smaller areas of cardiac muscle infarction than those undergoing sham treatment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r339">[339]</xref>&#x000a0;These benefits were independent of blood pressure effects.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r339">[339]</xref>&#x000a0;While preliminary, this investigational study suggests the potential benefit of renal denervation therapy in reducing the morbidity of myocardial infarctions in high cardiovascular risk populations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r339">[339]</xref></p>
        <p>Catheter-based renal denervation is unable to affect sympathetic nerves located &#x02265;4 mm from the renal artery. An experimental percutaneous microwave ablation technique that overcomes this problem has been used successfully in animals, with good results reported in preliminary studies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r340">[340]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Barriers to Implementing Institutional Renal Denervation Therapy Programs</bold>
</p>
        <p>There are several reasons why renal denervation therapy is not used more frequently.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r189">[189]</xref> Wider adoption of the procedure continues to face multiple obstacles, including the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Early flawed studies created the mistaken belief that the procedure delivers little benefit or only modest reductions in mean systolic blood pressure. This misconception continues to undermine clinician confidence. Therefore, persistent lack of confidence in the effectiveness of this procedure is a potential barrier for broader implementation.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Reported failure rates of renal denervation therapy approaching one-third also contribute to hesitation.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Additional training in the procedure is required for interventional clinicians.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Device movement may require frequent adjustments for optimal treatment delivery.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Many referred patients ultimately do not meet selection criteria, thereby further reducing procedural volume and slowing the development of broader clinical familiarity.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>FDA approval for renal denervation devices did not occur until November 2023, leaving the current experience limited.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Inconsistent insurance coverage across the United States continues to restrict access, although coverage trends are improving.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hospitals and medical centers frequently delay implementation because they lack the necessary equipment, are uncertain about device selection, or remain unwilling to purchase costly systems without reliable reimbursement and favorable cost-effectiveness analyses.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Uncertainty about which patients will achieve optimal benefit and which are likely to become nonresponders further complicates decision-making.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Incorrect assumptions that multiple medications (with their problems regarding cost, patient compliance, and&#x000a0;adverse effects) are superior to a procedural treatment, even if the procedure is proven safe, minimally invasive, without&#x000a0;adverse effects, permanent, and durable for improved blood pressure control.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Some clinicians continue to assume that extensive polypharmacy offers superior control compared to a procedural solution, even when the procedure demonstrates safety, minimal invasiveness, durability, and long-term effectiveness.&#x000a0;Limited awareness among clinicians regarding patient preference also restricts uptake; a substantial proportion of hypertensive patients (30%-40%) would choose a safe, minimally invasive procedure providing permanent blood pressure control over lifelong polypharmacy if offered that option.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r124">[124]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r125">[125]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r246">[246]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r341">[341]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r342">[342]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Clinicians frequently undervalue and undercommunicate the major advantages for patients with intractable hypertension, including permanent elimination of drug-drug interactions and the substantial adverse-effect burden associated with multiple potent antihypertensives.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r67">[67]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r162">[162]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r168">[168]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r343">[343]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>These adverse effects commonly include the following:&#x000a0;
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Cardiac arrhythmias</p></list-item><list-item><p>Chronic cough</p></list-item><list-item><p>Erectile dysfunction</p></list-item><list-item><p>Fatigue</p></list-item><list-item><p>Fluid retention</p></list-item><list-item><p>Electrolyte abnormalities</p></list-item><list-item><p>Gastrointestinal disturbances</p></list-item><list-item><p>Orthostatic hypotension</p></list-item><list-item><p>Peripheral edema</p></list-item><list-item><p>Sleep disorders</p></list-item><list-item><p>Urinary frequency&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r189">[189]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r344">[344]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r345">[345]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r346">[346]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r347">[347]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r348">[348]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Establishing an Institutional Renal Denervation Program&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>The Society for Cardiovascular Angiography &#x00026; Interventions (SCAI) has recommended the following criteria for establishing an institutional renal denervation program for intractable hypertension:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Any issues of patient noncompliance with their antihypertensive medications should be addressed before considering renal denervation therapy.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Medical clinician with special training, certification, or expertise in the management of hypertension.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>An institutional hypertension program. (American Heart Association Hypertension Center Certification is highly recommended, but is not required.)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Establishment of an interprofessional team to share in the evaluation and management of potential renal denervation patients. Such a team should include a patient navigator with expertise in hypertension, along with selected specialists in endocrinology, hypertension, cardiovascular medicine, interventional cardiology, interventional radiology, vascular medicine, and, if applicable, invasive/interventional nephrology.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Issues of patient noncompliance with their antihypertensive medications should be addressed before considering renal denervation therapy whenever possible. (Patients who fail to resolve compliance issues are still candidates for renal denervation therapy.)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Interventionalists should have adequate training and experience in renal denervation procedures, appropriate privileges, and should actively participate in presurgical decision-making and management.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Support staff should include nurses for home and ambulatory blood pressure monitoring, medical appointment coordination, a nutritionist, a pharmacist, and a hypertension nurse educator.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r67">[67]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r162">[162]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Responsibilities within the interprofessional team should be clearly defined, with each member contributing their specialized knowledge and skills to optimize patient care and management. Effective interprofessional communication fosters a collaborative environment where information is shared, questions are encouraged, and concerns are addressed promptly.</p>
        <p>Challenges in implementing renal denervation therapy include ensuring adequate interventionalist training and experience, especially in the early phases of adoption and in low-volume centers; ensuring equipment availability; establishing robust interprofessional teams for screening and patient selection protocols; maintaining procedural technical proficiency; and addressing the current lack of financial reimbursement for some patients in the United States.</p>
        <p>The lack of insurance coverage currently makes the procedure prohibitively expensive for many patients. Medicare has now begun covering the procedure, in accordance with the National Coverage Determination. Private payers will likely follow soon; however, the authorization rules and requirements remain unclear and are likely to continue to change.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-180794.s11" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Renal denervation therapy is an emerging intervention for patients with true drug-resistant or intractable hypertension who do not achieve adequate blood pressure control despite optimal pharmacologic therapy and assessment of adherence. Effective care begins with a precise diagnosis, including confirmation of resistant hypertension, evaluation for secondary causes, especially hyperaldosteronism, and careful patient selection.</p>
        <p>Primary care clinicians, internists, radiologists, cardiologists, nephrologists, hypertension specialists, and denervation therapy-trained interventional proceduralists work together to determine candidacy. At the same time, pharmacists, nurses, and advanced practitioners support medication optimization, patient education, follow-up, and longitudinal monitoring. SCAI recommends establishing multidisciplinary institutional programs with specially trained interventionalists and structured protocols, and has published specific criteria to ensure consistent evaluation, optimized patient selection, procedural safety, and standardized follow-up.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-180794.r162">[162]</xref></p>
        <p>High-quality, patient-centered care relies on clear roles, coordinated communication, and a shared strategy among all professionals involved in the management of hypertension. Clinicians must integrate evidence-based guidelines, screening pathways, and shared decision-making into individualized care plans. Nursing and support staff contribute through blood pressure monitoring, procedural preparation, and patient navigation, while pharmacists ensure medication adherence and identify interactions. Interprofessional teams review outcomes, refine screening criteria, and uphold program standards recommended by national societies. By strengthening collaborative care frameworks, enhancing clinician expertise, and improving access to renal denervation where appropriate, healthcare teams can reduce hypertension-related complications, improve safety, and elevate overall patient outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-180794.s12">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
